From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stage C <stage-c@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 21 Nov 2026 11:35:00 +1030
Subject: [PATCH] inline loop-contained calls the normal MIR inliner declines

Every call in the wasm we emit is expensive: arguments and results travel
through linear memory or a stack slot rather than registers, and the call
hides the callee's state from the later MIR passes. Iterator and string code
is a chain of small calls per element, so leaving a few of them out of line
keeps the iterator in memory and the per-element work in calls.

rustc's inliner already runs in the benchmark's warm compiles; passing
`-Zinline-mir=yes` produces byte-identical modules, so this pass cannot be
"turn inlining on". The problem is the size-based cost model, which is tuned
for LLVM: it declines the string iterator chain. `strings::body` is 1,676
MIR lines over 161 blocks by default, 3,171 at `-Zinline-mir-threshold=200`,
and 6,614 over 583 blocks at 400 as the chain is inlined. Threshold 100
leaves it unchanged, which is why the step-3 experiment saw no win there.

This pass reuses the inlining machinery and changes only the decision:

- a call site in a cyclic block of the caller gets a 200 cost threshold
  (`-Zmir-inline-wasm-threshold`), against the normal 50/100;
- a callee that takes a `&mut`/`*mut` argument gets 400, because the call
  forces the caller local behind that pointer into linear memory and inlining
  it is what lets reference propagation and SROA see the local again;
- calls outside loops are left to rustc's inliner, so cold code does not pay
  the code-size and compile-time cost;
- bodies without a back edge are skipped before the inliner runs;
- inlining stops once the caller has grown to four times its original block
  count.

Native MIR validation (stage-1 compiler, `--emit=obj`, wasm32 target):
`strings::body` 1,676 -> 3,441 lines over 335 blocks and `json` 1,183 ->
1,265; `scene`, `expr` and `collections` are unchanged at `body`.

Measured through the product pipeline with the rebuilt `rustc.wasm` on a
six-program subset (off against on, same compiler, 3 runs x 4 seeds; every
output identical):

| program | off | on | change | module | warm compile |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| `strings` | 107,244,701 | 66,008,021 | -38.4% | +2.07% | +6.2% |
| `collections` | 5,061,326 | 4,772,355 | -5.7% | -2.24% | +1.6% |
| `sort` | 321,207,695 | 299,459,431 | -6.8% | +0.05% | +3.6% |
| `json` | 1,871,062 | 1,777,296 | -5.0% | +1.87% | -1.6% |
| `nbody` | 6,204,745,840 | 6,204,745,840 | 0.0% | 0.0% | +2.3% |
| `int_loops` | 213,915 | 213,915 | 0.0% | 0.0% | +3.8% |

The numbers are executed wasm instructions from `bench/gap`, not timings.
`int_loops` is byte-identical and `nbody`'s module is byte-identical; their
small warm-compile deltas are the shared machine. The full difftest corpus is
green with the pass enabled.

The first revision of the patch used a 300 threshold for every loop call. It
gave `strings` the same time but inlined more (5,072 rather than 3,441 MIR
lines), grew modules (median +1.4%), cost +3.3% warm compile, and changed
`int_loops` enough to slow its 2-3 microsecond run by 38.7% in wall-clock
(despite 7.1% fewer instructions) -- a hot-loop layout effect. The 200 default
avoids that. Raising the whole threshold to 400 buys `strings` only another
-5% while growing `nbody`'s body from 144 to 264 blocks and its module by
10.6% for no executed-instruction change, so the extra budget is spent only on
callees that take a mutable pointer.

Third increment: what still lives in memory after the inliner.

Native final-MIR dumps of `strings`, `collections`, `sort`, `json` and
`nbody::body` (stage-1 compiler, `-Copt-level=2 -Zmir-opt-level=2
-Zmir-inline-wasm`) still showed 16, 36, 14, 23 and 16 address-taken locals.
The hot ones were all calls that borrow a caller local: `next_code_point(&mut
Iter<u8>)` in `strings` (cost 370, so declined at 200), `Zip`/`Chain`/
`vec_deque::Iter`/`Take<Skip>`/`btree_map::Iter::next` and `String::push` /
`VecDeque::push_back` / `BTreeSet::insert` in `collections`, and
`RawVec::grow_one` (`#[inline(never)]`) behind every `Vec` in `nbody`. SROA
cannot promote those locals: `rustc_mir_dataflow::value_analysis::
excluded_locals` excludes any local whose field address is observed, and
`Vec`/`String` also need a drop.

A second round of `ReferencePropagation -> SROA -> GVN` after inlining was
built and measured first, at two pipeline points. It left the hot locals in
place (strings 16 -> 16, sort 14 -> 14, json 23 -> 23, nbody 16 -> 16;
collections 36 -> 35) because the same `excluded_locals` rule still applies.
It was not kept.

The kept change is the mutable-pointer threshold above. On `strings` it
removes the hot-loop `SplitWhitespace` local whose `slice::Iter` was passed to
`next_code_point` (16 -> 15 address-taken locals; the call is inlined and the
iterator fields are read directly). On `collections` it removes two
address-taken locals (36 -> 34) from the inlined `BinaryHeap` and `BTreeSet`
paths. `sort`, `json` and `nbody` are unchanged in MIR shape, module size and
executed instructions, which is why this revision does not raise the whole
threshold.

The pass is opt-in through `-Zmir-inline-wasm`; the compiler, the sysroot and
the default benchmark builds do not pass it. This is a local CogWasm
experiment, not an upstream contribution.

